import{_ as s,o as t,c as a,an as i}from"./chunks/framework.DPv6r7OK.js";const n="/assets/examples_hpc_sizing.DG1rvWMS.png",u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Running on HPC","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"examples/hpc.md","filePath":"examples/hpc.md","lastUpdated":null}'),h={name:"examples/hpc.md"};function o(l,e,p,d,r,c){return t(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i(`<h1 id="Running-on-HPC" tabindex="-1">Running on HPC <a class="header-anchor" href="#Running-on-HPC" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Running on HPC {#Running-on-HPC}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The <code>hpc/</code> folder holds SLURM scripts for the examples. The 2-D cases are serial jobs; the 3-D plume-planform case is a two-rank MPI job that scales through threads. Everything a site has to change (account, partition, how Julia is put on the path) is marked <code>SITE</code> in the scripts.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">file</th><th style="text-align:left;">purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>hpc/plan_3d.jl</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">DOF count and per-rank memory estimate for each 3-D case</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>hpc/yinyang_convection_3d.slurm</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">array job over Ra = 10⁴, 10⁵, 10⁶ for <code>examples/yinyang_convection_3d.jl</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>hpc/submit_3d_chain.sh</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">submits that array as a chain of segments, each resuming the last</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>hpc/annulus_convection_2d.slurm</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">array job over Ra for <code>examples/annulus_convection_2d.jl</code></td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="Setting-up" tabindex="-1">Setting up <a class="header-anchor" href="#Setting-up" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Setting up {#Setting-up}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The source code is available from the author on request until it is released with a citable publication. In your copy of the repository, install the dependencies:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">cd</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> MILEVA</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">julia</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --project=.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -e</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()&#39;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">julia</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --project=.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -e</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;using MPI; MPI.install_mpiexecjl()&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">    # puts mpiexecjl in ~/.julia/bin</span></span></code></pre></div><p>By default MPI.jl uses the MPICH binary it ships. That is enough for these jobs, because the 3-D case runs two ranks on one node; the scripts launch it with <code>mpiexecjl</code>. To use the site MPI instead, switch MPI.jl to it once with <code>MPIPreferences.use_system_binary()</code> and submit with <code>MILEVA_LAUNCH=srun</code>, which starts the two ranks with <code>srun</code>.</p><h2 id="Sizing-the-3-D-cases" tabindex="-1">Sizing the 3-D cases <a class="header-anchor" href="#Sizing-the-3-D-cases" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Sizing the 3-D cases {#Sizing-the-3-D-cases}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The rank count is fixed. <code>solve_stokes_distributed!</code> (<code>solvers/solve_stokes_distributed.jl</code>) has one multigrid mode, Phase A, which puts the Yin patch on rank 0 and the Yang patch on rank 1; its multi-rank modes are MUMPS-direct or block-Jacobi Krylov, and MILEVA&#39;s rule is that 3-D Stokes is multigrid only. So each job is <strong>2 MPI ranks</strong>, and the knobs are the memory per rank and the threads per rank (<code>--cpus-per-task</code>, passed on as <code>JULIA_NUM_THREADS</code>; the SCGS smoother in <code>solvers/multigrid_stokes.jl</code> is threaded).</p><p><code>hpc/plan_3d.jl</code> prints the estimate for the default grids:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">julia</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --project=.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> hpc/plan_3d.jl</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> 32</span></span></code></pre></div><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>Ra     Nr    Nth   Nph      DOF/patch    DOF total    LU line    MG/rank    fits?</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>1e+04  64    32    96          795648      1591296      6.5 GB      1.3 GB      yes</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>1e+05  64    48    144        1785600      3571200     14.7 GB      2.9 GB      yes</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>1e+06  96    72    216        6008256     12016512     49.3 GB      9.6 GB      yes</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The DOF counts come from <code>estimate_dofs_raw</code> in <code>utils/dof_estimator.jl</code>. Its own Phase-A memory figure (&quot;LU line&quot;) assumes a direct LU factor of ten times the matrix, which the multigrid path never builds; it is shown for reference. The &quot;MG/rank&quot; column is the multigrid estimate: the fine operator, the Galerkin hierarchy (about one seventh of the fine operator in total), the FGMRES basis (restart 50) and the field arrays, with the estimator&#39;s constants of 50 nonzeros per row and 16 bytes per entry. Treat it as a lower bound. One data point from the laptop: during the first Stokes solve at the Ra = 10⁵ grid each rank held about 1.8 GB. The array script requests <code>--mem=48G</code> per job (two ranks), sized for the largest case with headroom, and a heap hint of 20 GB per rank.</p><div class="tip custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Estimate the wall time with a short probe</p><p>Run a five-step probe before a campaign and work out the wall time from the seconds per step it reports and the step count the CFL condition implies (the <a href="/examples/yinyang_plumes_3d#cost">3-D example page</a> has the details):</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">MILEVA_MAXSTEPS</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">5</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> MILEVA_LOGEVERY</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">1</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> sbatch</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --array=1</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --time=02:00:00</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> hpc/yinyang_convection_3d.slurm</span></span></code></pre></div></div><p><img src="`+n+`" alt=""></p><p><em>Unknowns and estimated memory per rank for the three default 3-D grids, from <code>estimate_dofs_raw</code> as printed by <code>hpc/plan_3d.jl</code>. The direct-LU line is shown for scale only; the multigrid path the driver uses fits a 32 GB node at every Rayleigh number.</em></p><h2 id="Submitting-the-3-D-sweep" tabindex="-1">Submitting the 3-D sweep <a class="header-anchor" href="#Submitting-the-3-D-sweep" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Submitting the 3-D sweep {#Submitting-the-3-D-sweep}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">sbatch</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> hpc/yinyang_convection_3d.slurm</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">            # one 48 h segment, all three Ra</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">sbatch</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --array=2</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> hpc/yinyang_convection_3d.slurm</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">  # Ra = 1e6 only</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">hpc/submit_3d_chain.sh</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> 6</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">                          # six chained segments</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">hpc/submit_3d_chain.sh</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> 6</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> 2</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --partition=long</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">       # Ra = 1e6, extra sbatch options</span></span></code></pre></div><p>What one array task does:</p><ol><li><p>picks <code>MILEVA_RA</code> from the array index (0, 1, 2 for 10⁴, 10⁵, 10⁶) and an output directory <code>output/hpc/yinyang_Ra&lt;Ra&gt;/</code> (base overridable with <code>MILEVA_OUTDIR_BASE</code>);</p></li><li><p>exits at once if that directory holds <code>FINISHED</code>;</p></li><li><p>sets <code>MILEVA_RESUME=latest</code>, so it continues from the newest <code>ckpt_rank&lt;r&gt;_step&lt;n&gt;.jld2</code> if there is one;</p></li><li><p>reads the job&#39;s remaining time with <code>squeue</code> and sets <code>MILEVA_WALLTIME_H</code> to 30 minutes less, so the driver checkpoints and stops before SLURM kills it;</p></li><li><p>launches two ranks with <code>JULIA_NUM_THREADS = SLURM_CPUS_PER_TASK</code> and <code>OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS = 1</code>.</p></li></ol><p><code>submit_3d_chain.sh N</code> queues N such jobs, each depending on the previous one with <code>afterany</code>, so a long run proceeds through the queue without attention. SLURM output goes to <code>logs/</code> in the submission directory.</p><h2 id="The-2-D-sweep" tabindex="-1">The 2-D sweep <a class="header-anchor" href="#The-2-D-sweep" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The 2-D sweep {#The-2-D-sweep}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">sbatch</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> hpc/annulus_convection_2d.slurm</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">             # Ra = 1e4, 1e5 at Nr = 64; 1e6 at Nr = 128; m = 6</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">NR</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">128</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> M</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">4</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> sbatch</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> hpc/annulus_convection_2d.slurm</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">  # other grid (all tasks) or planform</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Ra = 10⁶ defaults to Nr = 128 because at Nr = 64 its boundary layer is resolved by only a few cells: a Ra = 10⁶ run at Nr = 64 is a flow visualisation only, not a source of numbers.</p><p>Each task is one process with four threads, 16 GB (sized for the Nr = 128 task) and a 72 h limit. On the laptop, 40 steps at 64 × 512 took 0.41 s per step and 2.3 GB. The run length is set by the 0.3 diffusion-time cap and the CFL condition, so Ra = 10⁶ takes the most steps. <code>GKSwstype=nul</code> is set so figures render without a display. The plume and stagnant-lid examples need no script of their own: submit them the same way with the driver and arguments changed.</p><h2 id="Checks-done-on-these-scripts" tabindex="-1">Checks done on these scripts <a class="header-anchor" href="#Checks-done-on-these-scripts" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Checks done on these scripts {#Checks-done-on-these-scripts}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>All three shell scripts pass <code>bash -n</code>. <code>submit_3d_chain.sh</code> was dry-run with a stand-in <code>sbatch</code> and produced the expected dependency chain. <code>yinyang_convection_3d.slurm</code> was run outside SLURM on the laptop with a tiny grid (<code>MILEVA_NR=8 MILEVA_NTH=8 MILEVA_MGLEVELS=2 MILEVA_MAXSTEPS=2</code>), through <code>mpiexecjl</code>: it launched both ranks, finished, wrote <code>FINISHED</code>, and a second invocation skipped the case. None of them has yet run on a cluster.</p>`,27)])])}const g=s(h,[["render",o]]);export{u as __pageData,g as default};
